<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi again jpdelta6. Let’s take a look at your new concept.</p>
<p>Okay, I agree with everything <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grigori-karpin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4558017); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4558017&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606407482" alt="Grigori Karpin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4558017)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grigori-karpin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4558017); return false;">Grigori Karpin</a></span> is saying – basically, we need to work on building a narrative within your existing concept. Basically, you can’t just have a cool idea and inspiration for a scp these days – you have to show us how it works through the eyes of other characters within your article.</p>
<p>For example, what you’ve listed under “Central Narrative” is really you just describing the scp in more detail and how it functions. That’s not a story. A story involves a character encountering problems and trying to solve then. So, to give you an example:</p>
<p>“A shy college dormitory student discovers an unbranded cylinder Bluetooth stereo one day. He plays it in his room, and suddenly, he becomes the most popular student on campus, with people flocking to his room to party day and night… Little does he realise his strength fading day by day, and he starts to hear mysterious voices calling payment for his soul.”</p>
<p>Do you see how I’ve merely focused on a small aspect of your scip and turned it into a compelling discovery log? You can expand on this on choose another area to focus on. But we need to see a narrative in your articles before most of us are willing to greenlight concepts.</p>
<p>If you decide to revise the concept / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll do my best to take another look at it. For now, all the best!</p>

